<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 11: Complex Numbers and Damped Oscillations</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers sections 11.1, 11.2, and the complex numbers notes (or note 11.1
      if you have seen complex numbers already) 
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Equations 11.2 and 11.3 are equivalent, and example 1 makes it seem like it's pretty easy to find the 
            angle <m>\varphi</m>. Let's go through the following exercise:
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                       Take any values for <m>x_0,v_0,\omega</m> such that <m>x_0\lt 0,v_0\gt 0</m>
                        (write them in your answer). Find the angle <m>\varphi</m> using your calculator. 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use your value of <m>\varphi</m> to find the amplitude, and then recalculate
                        <m>x_0,v_0</m>. One of them should have the wrong sign.     
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Can you explain why the angle given by your calculator is wrong? Hint: look at the
                        graph of <m>\cos(x)</m> for <m>0\lt x \lt 2\pi</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Given what you found above, can you see a reason why it might be preferrable to use
                        equation 11.3 instead of equation 11.2 to describe harmonic motion?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        That said, if I'm going to give you the equation for <m>x(t)</m> in a problem directly
                        instead of having you find the constants, which form would you prefer?
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>  
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the complex numbers <m>z=1+i,w=3i,u=2\sqrt{3}-2i</m>.
          
          <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Draw these numbers as points in the plane, and find their magnitude and argument.
                    Use the range <m>-\pi \lt \theta \lt \pi</m> for the arguments.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Calculate <m>z+u,u-w,zw,z/u</m>
                </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>


  </page>
</worksheet>
